inct st.ticket_id. \"消息条数\" COUNT(tm.message_id). Potential issue if support_tickets ticket_id unique, left join. Good.\n\nNeed maybe count(*) for ticket count? Since each ticket can have multiple messages, count(*) would count messages. So count(distinct st.ticket_id). Since group by category/priority, each ticket in one group, but left join duplicates; distinct needed. Alternative preaggregate messages per ticket then join. But count(distinct) okay. Message count count(tm.message_id). Could include COUNT(tm.message_id) AS message_count.\n\nShould we order? \"按 category_id、priority组合统计\" Could order by category_id, priority maybe. But no explicit. Rule 11 no sort if not needed. Maybe grouping requires not sort. But many SQL answers use ORDER BY. To comply, omit order. LIMIT 1000? Need limit. Perhaps LIMIT 1000. But if there are more combos, not all. Hard rule says must LIMIT. Could LIMIT 2147483647? It violates spirit? Need LIMIT. Since \"全部工单\" means all tickets within groups, not necessarily all groups? The grouping combos may be many. Need return all groups. We can use LIMIT 1000? There are category_id and priority combos finite: category count unknown maybe 10 and priority 4 => 40. So LIMIT 1000 won't cut. Good.\n\nNeed no code fence, no explanation. Single SELECT. Could include comment? Not necessary. Ensure semicolon? okay. Use LIMIT 1000. Does \"包含零消息工单及未知属性\" unknown attributes means category_id/priority null. left join from support_tickets. No filter.\n\nNeed maybe \"消息条数\" per group counts messages, but if ticket no messages, 0. COUNT(tm.message_id) yields 0. Good.\n\nPotential subtle: if ticket_messages has message_id but maybe sender_type etc. Use count(tm.message_id). Good.\n\nLet's output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2927,"completion_tokens":880,"total_tokens":3807,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":805},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss